# Laravel Clean Architecture (DDD Approach)

This project follows clean architecture principles using Laravel DDD patterns.

## Tech Stack

- **Laravel 12** - PHP framework
- **Laravel Data** - Type-safe DTOs
- **TypeScript Transformer** - Generate TypeScript types from PHP
- **InertiaJS** - Modern monolith approach
- **Socialite** - OAuth authentication

## Architecture Layers

### Domain Layer (app/Models/, app/Data/)
- **Models**: Eloquent models representing business entities
- **Data**: Laravel Data objects (immutable DTOs)

### Application Layer (app/Actions/)
- **Actions**: Single-purpose business logic classes
- Use case implementations
- Invokable classes that orchestrate domain logic

### Infrastructure Layer (app/Services/, app/Repositories/)
- **Services**: External integrations (OAuth, APIs, third-party services)
- **Repositories**: Data access patterns (optional, use when needed)

### Presentation Layer (app/Http/)
- **Controllers**: Thin layer that delegates to Actions
- **Middleware**: Request/response processing
- **Requests**: Form validation classes
- **Resources**: API response formatting (if needed)

## Coding Standards

### Controllers (Thin Layer)

Controllers should be thin and delegate to Actions:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Thin controller
class OAuthController extends Controller
{
    public function __construct(
        private CreateUserFromOAuth $createUser
    ) {}

    public function handleCallback()
    {
        $oauthUser = Socialite::driver('google')->user();
        $userData = ($this->createUser)($oauthUser->getRaw());

        Auth::loginUsingId($userData->id);

        return Inertia::render('Dashboard');
    }
}

// ❌ BAD - Fat controller with business logic
class OAuthController extends Controller
{
    public function handleCallback()
    {
        $oauthUser = Socialite::driver('google')->user();

        // Business logic in controller (BAD)
        $user = User::where('google_id', $oauthUser->getId())->first();
        if (!$user) {
            $user = new User();
            $user->google_id = $oauthUser->getId();
        }
        $user->name = $oauthUser->getName();
        $user->email = $oauthUser->getEmail();
        $user->save();

        Auth::login($user);
        return Inertia::render('Dashboard');
    }
}
```

### Actions (Business Logic)

Actions should be single-purpose, invokable classes:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Action with single responsibility
namespace App\Actions\Auth;

use App\Data\UserData;
use App\Models\User;

class CreateUserFromOAuth
{
    public function __invoke(array $oauthData): UserData
    {
        $user = User::updateOrCreate(
            ['google_id' => $oauthData['id']],
            [
                'name' => $oauthData['name'],
                'email' => $oauthData['email'],
                'avatar' => $oauthData['avatar'],
                'email_verified_at' => now(),
            ]
        );

        return UserData::from($user);
    }
}

// ❌ BAD - Action doing multiple things
class HandleOAuthLogin
{
    public function execute($provider)
    {
        // Too many responsibilities
        $user = $this->createUser();
        $this->sendWelcomeEmail($user);
        $this->logUserIn($user);
        $this->trackAnalytics($user);
        return $user;
    }
}
```

### Data Classes (DTOs)

Always use Laravel Data with TypeScript generation:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Data class with TypeScript generation
namespace App\Data;

use Spatie\LaravelData\Data;
use Spatie\TypeScriptTransformer\Attributes\TypeScript;

#[TypeScript]
class UserData extends Data
{
    public function __construct(
        public int $id,
        public string $name,
        public string $email,
        public ?string $avatar,
    ) {}
}

// ❌ BAD - Using plain arrays
public function getUser(): array
{
    return [
        'id' => $user->id,
        'name' => $user->name,
        'email' => $user->email,
    ];
}

// ❌ BAD - Missing TypeScript attribute
class UserData extends Data
{
    public function __construct(
        public int $id,
        public string $name,
    ) {}
}
```

### Models (Domain Entities)

Keep models clean and focused:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Clean model
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Foundation\Auth\User as Authenticatable;

class User extends Authenticatable
{
    protected $fillable = [
        'name',
        'email',
        'password',
        'google_id',
        'avatar',
    ];

    protected $hidden = [
        'password',
        'remember_token',
    ];

    protected function casts(): array
    {
        return [
            'email_verified_at' => 'datetime',
            'password' => 'hashed',
        ];
    }
}

// ❌ BAD - Model with business logic
class User extends Authenticatable
{
    public function createFromOAuth($oauthData)
    {
        // Business logic should be in Actions
        $this->name = $oauthData['name'];
        $this->save();
        $this->sendWelcomeEmail();
    }
}
```

## Type Safety Requirements

### Always Use Type Declarations

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Typed properties and return types
class CreatePost
{
    public function __invoke(array $data): PostData
    {
        $post = Post::create($data);
        return PostData::from($post);
    }
}

// ❌ BAD - No type declarations
class CreatePost
{
    public function __invoke($data)
    {
        $post = Post::create($data);
        return $post->toArray();
    }
}
```

### Use Laravel Data for DTOs

Always return Data objects, not arrays:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Return Data objects
public function index(): Response
{
    $posts = Post::all();
    return Inertia::render('Posts/Index', [
        'posts' => PostData::collection($posts),
    ]);
}

// ❌ BAD - Return raw models or arrays
public function index(): Response
{
    $posts = Post::all();
    return Inertia::render('Posts/Index', [
        'posts' => $posts->toArray(),
    ]);
}
```

## InertiaJS Patterns

### Controllers Return Inertia

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Return Inertia response
public function show(Post $post): Response
{
    return Inertia::render('Posts/Show', [
        'post' => PostData::from($post),
    ]);
}

// ❌ BAD - Return JSON or view
public function show(Post $post)
{
    return response()->json($post);
}
```

### Share Data via Middleware

Use HandleInertiaRequests for shared data:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Share auth data in middleware
public function share(Request $request): array
{
    return [
        ...parent::share($request),
        'auth' => [
            'user' => $request->user()
                ? UserData::from($request->user())
                : null,
        ],
    ];
}
```

## Directory Structure

Follow this structure for clean architecture:

```
app/
├── Actions/              # Business logic (use cases)
│   ├── Auth/
│   │   ├── CreateUserFromOAuth.php
│   │   └── LogoutUser.php
│   └── Posts/
│       ├── CreatePost.php
│       └── UpdatePost.php
├── Data/                 # DTOs with TypeScript generation
│   ├── UserData.php
│   └── PostData.php
├── Http/
│   ├── Controllers/      # Thin controllers
│   │   └── Auth/
│   │       └── OAuthController.php
│   ├── Middleware/
│   │   └── HandleInertiaRequests.php
│   └── Requests/         # Form validation
│       └── StorePostRequest.php
├── Models/               # Eloquent models
│   ├── User.php
│   └── Post.php
└── Services/             # External services
    ├── GoogleOAuthService.php
    └── NotificationService.php
```

## Dependency Injection

Use constructor injection for dependencies:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Constructor injection
class PostController extends Controller
{
    public function __construct(
        private CreatePost $createPost,
        private UpdatePost $updatePost
    ) {}

    public function store(StorePostRequest $request)
    {
        $postData = ($this->createPost)($request->validated());
        return redirect()->route('posts.show', $postData->id);
    }
}

// ❌ BAD - Direct instantiation
class PostController extends Controller
{
    public function store(Request $request)
    {
        $action = new CreatePost();
        $post = $action->execute($request->all());
        return redirect()->back();
    }
}
```

## TypeScript Generation

Always add `#[TypeScript]` to Data classes that will be used in the frontend:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - TypeScript attribute for frontend use
#[TypeScript]
class PostData extends Data
{
    public function __construct(
        public int $id,
        public string $title,
        public string $content,
        public UserData $author,
        public Carbon $created_at,
    ) {}
}

// ❌ BAD - Missing TypeScript attribute
class PostData extends Data
{
    // Frontend won't have type definitions
    public function __construct(
        public int $id,
        public string $title,
    ) {}
}
```

After creating or modifying Data classes, run:
```bash
npm run generate-types
```

## Database Prefix

This project uses table prefix `ln_`. Laravel handles this automatically, but for raw queries:

```php
// ✅ GOOD - Prefix handled automatically
$users = User::all();
$sessions = DB::table('sessions')->get();

// ✅ GOOD - Raw query with prefix helper
DB::select('SELECT * FROM ' . DB::getTablePrefix() . 'users');

// ❌ BAD - Raw query without prefix
DB::select('SELECT * FROM users');
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

1. **Fat Controllers**: Business logic should be in Actions, not Controllers
2. **Direct DB Queries in Controllers**: Use Actions or Models
3. **Missing Type Declarations**: Always declare parameter and return types
4. **Plain Arrays Instead of Data Objects**: Use Laravel Data for type safety
5. **Business Logic in Models**: Keep models focused on data representation
6. **Missing TypeScript Attributes**: Frontend needs generated types
7. **Tight Coupling**: Use dependency injection, not direct instantiation

## Summary

- Keep controllers thin - delegate to Actions
- Use Laravel Data for all DTOs - add `#[TypeScript]` attribute
- Create Actions for business logic - single responsibility
- Use dependency injection for testability
- Declare all types for better IDE support and type safety
- Follow the defined directory structure
